%U A072142 114396678,139703058,195802398,239801958,305801397,326692377,332198856,424604466,446604246,487307106,619306974
%N A072142 Numbers k such that 14 applications of 'Reverse and Subtract' lead to k, whereas fewer than 14 applications do not lead to k.
%C A072142 There are 14 eight-digit terms in the sequence. Further terms are obtained (a) by inserting at the center of these terms either any number of 9's (for 11436678, 32662377, 33218856, 76226733) or any number of 0's (for the other ten terms) and (b) by concatenating a term any number of times with itself and inserting an equal number of 0's at all junctures. Method (b) may be applied recursively to all terms. - Revised thanks to a comment from Hans Havermann, Jan 20 2004.
%C A072142 Not all terms are obtained from the 8-digit terms by the two constructions above. A new cycle, and therefore new terms, appears with 70 digits (smallest term: 1427103049577240250574242604301814185728964948574258380563339506981858; it is a doubling, see A215669); further new cycles appear with 74, 100, 112, 114, 116, 128, 130, 138, 142 and 148 digits, among others. - _Frédéric Fichepain_, Nov 06 2026
%H A072142 Frédéric Fichepain, <a href="/A072142/b072142.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..700</a>
%H A072142 Frédéric Fichepain, <a href="/A072142/a072142.txt">Digit-pair automaton for the 14-cycles: description and program</a>
%F A072142 k = f^14(k), k <> f^m(k) for m < 14, where f: x -> |x - reverse(x)|.
%e A072142 11436678 -> 76226733 -> 42464466 -> 23981958 -> 61936974 -> 13973058 -> 71064873 -> 33218856 -> 32662377 -> 44664246 -> 19582398 -> 69746193 -> 30581397 -> 48737106 -> 11436678.
